1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,450
1. Introduction.

2
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:11,862
Commerce on the Internet has come to rely almost exclusively on financial institutions serving as trusted third parties to process electronic payments.

3
00:00:11,862 --> 00:00:19,500
While the system works well enough for most  transactions,  it  still  suffers  from  the  inherent  weaknesses  of  the  trust  based  model.

4
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:27,812
Completely non-reversible transactions are not really possible, since financial institutions cannot avoid  mediating  disputes.

5
00:00:27,812 --> 00:00:44,550
The  cost  of  mediation  increases  transaction  costs,  limiting  the minimum practical transaction size and cutting off the possibility for small casual transactions, and there is a broader cost in the loss of ability to make non-reversible payments for non- reversible services.

6
00:00:44,550 --> 00:00:48,850
With the possibility of reversal, the need for trust spreads.

7
00:00:48,850 --> 00:00:55,462
Merchants must be wary of their customers, hassling them for more information than they would otherwise need.

8
00:00:55,462 --> 00:00:59,500
A certain percentage of fraud is accepted as unavoidable.

9
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:10,825
These costs and payment uncertainties can be avoided in person by using physical currency, but no mechanism exists to make payments over a communications channel without a trusted party.

10
00:01:10,825 --> 00:01:22,750
What is needed is an electronic payment system based on cryptographic proof instead of trust, allowing any two willing parties to transact directly with each other without the need for a trusted third party.

11
00:01:22,750 --> 00:01:33,275
Transactions that are computationally impractical to reverse would protect sellers from fraud, and routine escrow mechanisms could easily be implemented to protect buyers.

12
00:01:33,275 --> 00:01:44,987
In this paper, we propose a solution to the double-spending problem using a peer-to-peer distributed timestamp server to generate computational proof of the chronological order of transactions.

13
00:01:44,987 --> 00:01:53,000
The system  is  secure  as  long  as  honest  nodes  collectively  control  more  CPU  power  than  any cooperating group of attacker nodes.

14
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:54,362
